<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Manchuria Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as South Manchuria Railway Company, Mantetsu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> Railway Company was a Japanese semi-official company founded in 1906 to run the railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalian">Dairen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushunkou">Port Arthur</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a>. Based at Dairen, it also ran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines such as Fushun, ports, hotels, towns and a large research bureau, and became the chief instrument of Japanese influence in Manchuria. A staged bombing of its line near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a> in 1931 gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> a pretext to seize the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-manchuria-railway.jpg" alt="South Manchuria Railway" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MasakiTomasawa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_mark_of_South_Manchuria_Railway.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
